{
  "conversation_lines": [
    {
      "turn_number": 1,
      "time": "2173-05-26 19:04:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Hi, I'm Dr. Lee. I understand you've been feeling confused and having low blood sugar episodes. Can you tell me more about what’s been going on?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 2,
      "time": "2173-05-26 19:05:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Yes, thank you. I’ve been tired, sweating a lot, and confused—especially after eating. I think it’s because my body isn’t absorbing my meds or nutrients right after my gastric bypass."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 3,
      "time": "2173-05-26 19:06:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "That makes sense. You mentioned your Seroquel levels were low despite high doses. We’ll check your absorption and run some labs to see what’s going on."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 4,
      "time": "2173-05-26 19:07:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "And my ammonia was high two weeks ago. They gave me Rifaximin, but I’m still not feeling right."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 5,
      "time": "2173-05-26 19:08:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We’ve reviewed that. Your initial ammonia was normal today, but we’ll monitor it closely. We’ll also check liver function and look for metabolic causes."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 6,
      "time": "2173-05-26 19:09:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Could this confusion be from my liver? I’ve had fatty liver in the past."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 7,
      "time": "2173-05-26 19:10:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "It’s possible, but your liver enzymes are normal now. We’ll get a full workup, including an MRI and EEG, to rule out other causes like seizures or brain lesions."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 8,
      "time": "2173-05-26 19:11:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Okay, that’s reassuring. What about my low blood sugar?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 9,
      "time": "2173-05-26 19:12:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Endocrinology will evaluate that. We suspect it might be due to overproduction of insulin after meals, which can happen after gastric bypass."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 10,
      "time": "2173-05-26 19:13:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "So not just from my tube feeds being off?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 11,
      "time": "2173-05-26 19:14:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We’ll keep your tube feeds running for now. But we may do a fasting test to see how your body handles low intake."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 12,
      "time": "2173-05-27 08:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Good morning. Your ammonia level rose to 52 yesterday. We’re concerned this could be contributing to your confusion."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 13,
      "time": "2173-05-27 08:01:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Is that dangerous? What causes that?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 14,
      "time": "2173-05-27 08:02:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "High ammonia can affect brain function. It’s often from liver issues, but your liver looks okay. We’re considering a rare genetic issue in your metabolism."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 15,
      "time": "2173-05-27 08:03:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "A genetic problem? Like something I was born with?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 16,
      "time": "2173-05-27 08:04:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Possibly. We’re sending a urea cycle panel after a 12-hour fast to check for that. It’s rare, but it fits your symptoms after high-protein meals."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 17,
      "time": "2173-05-27 08:05:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I do feel worse after eating meat. So that could be it?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 18,
      "time": "2173-05-27 08:06:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Exactly. We’ll test that theory. For now, we’ll keep you on clear liquids and continue your tube feeds."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 19,
      "time": "2173-05-28 06:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We’re going to hold your tube feeds overnight for a fasting test. Endocrinology wants to check your insulin levels when you’re not eating."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 20,
      "time": "2173-05-28 06:01:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Will I get low blood sugar again?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 21,
      "time": "2173-05-28 06:02:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We’ll monitor you closely. If your glucose drops, we’ll treat it right away. This test helps us see if your body is making too much insulin."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 22,
      "time": "2173-05-28 06:03:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Okay, I trust you. But please wake me if I seem confused."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 23,
      "time": "2173-05-29 07:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Good news—you didn’t become hypoglycemic overnight. Your insulin, C-peptide, and beta-hydroxybutyrate were normal."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 24,
      "time": "2173-05-29 07:01:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "So it’s not my insulin causing lows?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 25,
      "time": "2173-05-29 07:02:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Not during fasting. But it could still be happening after meals. Endocrinology recommends continuing continuous tube feeds and avoiding food by mouth."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 26,
      "time": "2173-05-29 07:03:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "So no eating at all? Not even a cracker?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 27,
      "time": "2173-05-29 07:04:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Nothing with calories. Water is fine. Eating could trigger dumping syndrome or insulin spikes. We want to keep your glucose stable."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 28,
      "time": "2173-05-29 10:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Hepatology saw you today. They don’t think you have cirrhosis or hepatic encephalopathy."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 29,
      "time": "2173-05-29 10:01:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "So what’s causing the confusion?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 30,
      "time": "2173-05-29 10:02:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "They think it’s related to your gastric bypass anatomy. A high-protein meal may overwhelm your metabolism, especially if there’s a urea cycle issue."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 31,
      "time": "2173-05-30 09:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We’re going to do a test today. You’ll have a high-protein meal, and we’ll draw blood every 30 minutes to check ammonia and liver enzymes."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 32,
      "time": "2173-05-30 09:01:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Will I feel bad again?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 33,
      "time": "2173-05-30 09:02:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You might feel some symptoms, like confusion or nausea, but we’ll be right here. It’s important we capture what happens."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 34,
      "time": "2173-05-30 12:30:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "After your meal, your AST and ALT went up, and your ammonia peaked at 52. You had slurred speech and tremors, but they improved."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 35,
      "time": "2173-05-30 12:31:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I don’t remember much after lunch. That scares me."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 36,
      "time": "2173-05-30 12:32:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "That’s called transient confusion. It’s likely due to the protein load. The good news is your MRI and EEG were normal—no seizures or brain lesions."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 37,
      "time": "2173-05-30 12:33:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "So it’s not my brain, it’s my metabolism?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 38,
      "time": "2173-05-30 12:34:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Exactly. We’re waiting on the urea cycle panel. If that’s normal, we’ll consider other metabolic pathways."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 39,
      "time": "2173-05-31 08:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Your discharge is coming up. Your ammonia is down to 23, and you’ve been stable."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 40,
      "time": "2173-05-31 08:01:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Are we any closer to an answer?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 41,
      "time": "2173-05-31 08:02:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We think your symptoms are due to post-gastric bypass changes. The high-protein meal test confirmed that. A genetic urea cycle defect is still possible—results are pending."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 42,
      "time": "2173-05-31 08:03:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "And the low blood sugar?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 43,
      "time": "2173-05-31 08:04:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Endocrinology thinks it’s dumping syndrome—rapid food movement into the intestine causing insulin surge. That’s why we’re keeping you on tube feeds only."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 44,
      "time": "2173-05-31 08:05:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "What about my meds? I take Rifaximin for bacterial overgrowth."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 45,
      "time": "2173-05-31 08:06:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You’ll continue Rifaximin three times a day. We don’t think SIBO is the main issue, but it’s safe to keep it for now."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 46,
      "time": "2173-06-01 09:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Your liver team recommends switching your tube feed formula to NutriHep, which is easier on your metabolism."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 47,
      "time": "2173-06-01 09:01:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Will that help with the confusion?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 48,
      "time": "2173-06-01 09:02:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "It’s designed for people with liver or metabolic issues. It has balanced protein and calories to avoid overwhelming your system."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 49,
      "time": "2173-06-01 09:03:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "And when will I get the new formula?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 50,
      "time": "2173-06-01 09:04:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "It’s being arranged. Until then, stay on your current feeds at 65cc/hr. You have follow-ups with your PCP, endocrinologist, and surgeon."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 51,
      "time": "2173-06-02 10:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You’re doing well. We’re discharging you today. Continue tube feeds, no oral intake, and follow up in one week."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 52,
      "time": "2173-06-02 10:01:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Thank you. I feel better already."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 53,
      "time": "2173-06-02 10:02:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We’ll call you when the urea cycle and pro-insulin results come in. Call us if you have confusion, low sugar, or can’t keep feeds down."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 54,
      "time": "2173-06-02 10:03:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I will. And I’ll keep my appointments."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 55,
      "time": "2173-06-02 10:04:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Great. Take care, and we’ll be in touch."
    }
  ],
  "summary": {
    "admission_start": "2173-05-26 19:04:00",
    "admission_end": "2173-06-02 14:52:00",
    "summary_paragraph": "The patient was admitted for confusion and hypoglycemia following Roux-en-Y gastric bypass. Initial evaluation revealed normal liver function and no structural brain abnormalities on MRI and EEG. Ammonia levels fluctuated, peaking at 52, with post-prandial confusion and tremors after a high-protein meal, suggesting a possible urea cycle disorder. Endocrinology evaluated hyperinsulinemic hypoglycemia but found normal insulin and C-peptide during fasting; symptoms were attributed to dumping syndrome. The patient remained on continuous tube feeds, avoided oral intake, and was transitioned to NutriHep formula. Rifaximin was continued, and follow-up was arranged for pending urea cycle and pro-insulin results. She improved clinically and was discharged home in stable condition.",
    "problems": [
      "Other complications of other bariatric procedure",
      "Disorders of urea cycle metabolism",
      "Other chronic nonalcoholic liver disease",
      "Delirium due to conditions classified elsewhere",
